  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\n| | 0 | 1 | _2_ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| top | /a | | /b |\n| frames[0] | /i-a | /i-b | |\n\n```\ndata:text/html,<iframe id=i></iframe><button onclick=\"i.src = 'https://example.com'\">click</button><button onclick=\"location = 'https://example.org'\">away</button>\n```\n\nWhat should happen for `history.go(-1)`?\n\n---\n\n[apply the history step](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#apply-the-history-step) determines `changingNavigables` as [the navigables that change or reload](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#get-all-navigables-whose-current-session-history-entry-will-change-or-reload) and because top changes, `navigablesToCheck` isn't extended with the child navigable. Even if the child were in `changingNavigables`, they are torn down anyway. It seems like there is a todo about this:\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#nested-history\n> This will later contain ways to identify a child navigable across reloads.\n\nBrowsers instead restore the `i-b` entry.",
